{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793973112,"nonce":"eepsKGMORvEANWnS8ZBmBazAnag=","title":"An id :hover or :active rule is applied before complex rules, so at equal specificity it beats a later :checked rule","message":"With `#cb:hover { background: #336699 } #cb:checked { background: #228844 }` and a checked, hovered box, a browser draws #228844 (same specificity, the later rule wins). Blinc draws #336699.\n\nCause: `#id:hover`, `:active`, `:focus` and `:focus-visible` are still stored as simple pointer-state rules and applied by `apply_state_styles`; every complex rule, including the `#id:checked` and `#id:disabled` rules that a5de3bd moved there, is applied after them. Order between the two kinds is therefore fixed (simple, then complex) instead of by specificity and source order. The same split makes an id `:hover` rule lose to a complex rule it outranks, for example the widgets' own `.blinc-checkbox:hover:not(:disabled):not(:checked) { border-color }` against an app `#cb:hover { border-color }`; the \"core under hover and checked rules\" scenario in crates/blinc_cn/tests/checkbox_states.golden shows the hover border as the widget's, not the app's.\n\nOptions: store every `#id:state` as a complex rule (one ordering, loses the pre-resolved state table's speed), or apply the simple layers again after the complex pass when their specificity is higher. The golden records today's output; re-bless it with the change.","files":null}]}